[46.920644, "o", "nl -ba docs/spec_v1.md | sed -n '1,120p'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[46.924058, "o", "     1\t# ML Evaluation Report Engine - Specification v1\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\t## 1. Overview\r\n     4\t\r\n     5\t`mlreport` is a command-line tool that processes raw ML model evaluation data, computes\r\n"]
[46.924179, "o", "     6\tclassification and regression metrics, generates structured reports, and detects\r\n     7\tperformance regressions between model versions.\r\n     8\t\r\n     9\tAll computation rules, input/output schemas, and CLI semantics are defined in this\r\n    10\tdocument. The engine must support three task types: **binary classification**,\r\n    11\t**multiclass classification**, and **regression**.\r\n    12\t\r\n    13\t---\r\n    14\t\r\n    15\t## 2. Data Model\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\t### 2.1 Evaluation Manifest\r\n    18\t\r\n    19\tAn evaluation manifest is a JSON file with the following structure:\r\n    20\t\r\n    21\t```json\r\n    22\t{\r\n    23\t  \"model\": {\r\n    24\t    \"name\": \"<string>\",\r\n    25\t    \"version\": \"<string>\",\r\n    26\t    \"task_type\": \"<binary_classification | multiclass_classification | regression>\"\r\n"]
[46.924305, "o", "    27\t  },\r\n    28\t  \"evaluations\": [\r\n    29\t    {\r\n    30\t      \"split\": \"<string>\",\r\n    31\t      \"dataset\": \"<string>\",\r\n    32\t      \"predictions\": [\r\n    33\t        {\r\n    34\t          \"id\": \"<string>\","]
[46.924379, "o", "\r\n    35\t          \"actual\": \"<label_string_or_number>\",\r\n    36\t          \"predicted\": \"<label_string_or_number>\",\r\n    37\t          \"probabilities\": { \"<class_label>\": <float>, ... }\r\n    38\t        }\r\n    39\t      ]\r\n    40\t    }\r\n    41\t  ]\r\n    42\t}\r\n    43\t```\r\n    44\t\r\n    45\tField semantics:\r\n    46\t\r\n    47\t- `model.name`: Model identifier (alphanumeric + hyphens).\r\n    48\t- `model.version`: Semantic version string.\r\n    49\t- `model.task_type`: One of `binary_classification`, `multiclass_classification`, `regression`.\r\n    50\t- `evaluations`: Array of evaluation runs, one per dataset split.\r\n    51\t- `evaluations[].split`: Split name (e.g., `\"test\"`, `\"validation\"`).\r\n    52\t- `evaluations[].dataset`: Dataset identifier.\r\n    53\t- `evaluations[].predictions`: Array of individual predictions.\r\n    54\t- `predictions[].id`: Unique sample identifier within the split.\r\n    55\t- `predictions[].actual`: Ground truth label (string for classification, number for regression).\r\n    56\t- `predictions[].predicted"]
[46.924566, "o", "`: Model prediction (string for classification, number for regression).\r\n    57\t- `predictions[].probabilities`: Class probability distribution (classification only). Keys are class labels, values are floats in [0,1] summing to ~1.0. Must be present for all classification predictions.\r\n    58\t\r\n    59\t**Validation rules:**\r\n    60\t- All required fields must be present.\r\n    61\t- `task_type` must be one of the three valid values.\r\n    62\t- For classification: `actual` and `predicted` must be strings; `probabilities` must be present.\r\n    63\t- For regression: `actual` and `predicted` must be numbers; `probabilities` must be absent or null.\r\n    64\t- Each split must have at least one prediction.\r\n    65\t- Prediction IDs must be unique within a split.\r\n    66\t\r\n    67\t### 2.2 Regression Configuration\r\n    68\t\r\n    69\tUsed with the `compare` command to define regression thresholds and rules:\r\n    70\t\r\n    71\t```json\r\n    72\t{\r\n    73\t  \"thresholds\": {\r\n    74\t    \"<metric_name>\": {\r\n    75\t      \"min_absolute_delt"]
[46.924692, "o", "a\": <float>,\r\n    76\t      \"min_relative_delta\": <float>,\r\n    77\t      \"severity\": \"<critical | warning | info>\"\r\n    78\t    }\r\n    79\t  },\r\n    80\t  \"rules\": {\r\n    81\t    \"max_critical_regressions\": <int>,\r\n    82\t    \"max_warning_regressions\": <int>\r\n    83\t  }\r\n    84\t}\r\n    85\t```\r\n    86\t\r\n    87\tField semantics:\r\n    88\t\r\n    89\t- `thresholds.<metric>`: Defines the acceptable change for a specific metric.\r\n    90\t- `min_absolute_delta`: Minimum acceptable absolute change (candidate - baseline). Example: `-0.02` means the metric may drop by at most 0.02.\r\n    91\t- `min_relative_delta`: Minimum acceptable relative change as a fraction. Example: `-0.05` means the metric may drop by at most 5% relative to baseline. Computed as `(candidate - baseline) / |baseline|` when `|baseline| > 1e-10`, otherwise relative check is skipped.\r\n    92\t- `severity`: How critical this metric is.\r\n    93\t- A metric is considered **regressed** if its actual delta violates **either** the absolute **or** relative threshold (whi"]
[46.924809, "o", "chever is stricter).\r\n    94\t- `rules.max_critical_regressions`: Maximum number of critical-severity metrics that can regress. Exceeding this count -> overall FAIL.\r\n    95\t- `rules.max_warning_regressions`: Maximum number of warning-severity metrics that can regress. Exceeding this count -> overall FAIL.\r\n    96\t- Info-severity regressions are reported but never cause overall FAIL.\r\n    97\t- If a metric appears in evaluation results but not in `thresholds`, it is ignored for regression purposes.\r\n    98\t- If a metric appears in `thresholds` but not in evaluation results, it is ignored.\r\n    99\t\r\n   100\t### 2.3 Per-Metric Regression Evaluation\r\n   101\t\r\n   102\tFor each split in both baseline and candidate manifests:\r\n   103\t\r\n   104\t1. Compute all metrics for both baseline and candidate on that split.\r\n   105\t2. For each metric defined in `thresholds`:\r\n   106\t   a. Compute `absolute_delta = candidate_value - baseline_value`.\r\n   107\t   b. Compute `relative_delta = (candidate_value - baseline_value) / |baseli"]
[46.924946, "o", "ne_value|` if `|baseline_value| > 1e-10`, else skip relative check.\r\n   108\t   c. The metric is regressed on this split if `absolute_delta < min_absolute_delta` OR `relative_delta < min_relative_delta` (when relative check is applicable).\r\n   109\t3. A metric is counted toward the severity bucket if it regresses on **any** split.\r\n   110\t4. Each metric counts at most once toward its severity bucket regardless of how many splits it regresses on.\r\n   111\t\r\n   112\t---\r\n   113\t\r\n   114\t## 3. Metric Computation\r\n   115\t\r\n   116\t### 3.1 Binary Classification\r\n   117\t\r\n   118\t**Positive class convention:** The positive class is the class label that comes **last** in case-insensitive lexicographic order. For example, between `\"negative\"` and `\"positive\"`, `\"positive\"` is the positive class. Between `\"benign\"` and `\"malignant\"`, `\"malignant\"` is positive.\r\n   119\t\r\n   120\t**Confusion matrix** from raw predictions using the `predicted` field:\r\n"]
[46.925388, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[46.925484, "o", "root@n30009:/app# "]
[47.196108, "i", "nl -ba docs/spec_v1.md | sed -n '121,240p'\n"]
[47.196451, "o", "nl -ba docs/spec_v1.md | sed -n '121,240p'\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[47.199771, "o", "   121\t- TP: `actual == positive_class AND predicted == positive_class`\r\n   122\t- FP: `actual == negative_class AND predicted == positive_class`\r\n   123\t- FN: `actual == positive_class AND predicted == negative_class`\r\n   124\t- TN: `actual == negative_class AND predicted == negative_class`\r\n   125\t"]
[47.199911, "o", "\r\n   126\t**Metrics** (all rounded to 6 decimal places in output):\r\n   127\t\r\n   128\t| Metric | Formula | Division-by-zero handling |\r\n   129\t|--------|---------|--------------------------|\r\n   130\t| `accuracy` | `(TP + TN) / (TP + TN + FP + FN)` | Return 0.0 if denominator is 0 |\r\n   131\t| `precision` | `TP / (TP + FP)` | Return 0.0 if denominator is 0 |\r\n   132\t| `recall` | `TP / (TP + FN)` | Return 0.0 if denominator is 0 |\r\n   133\t| `f1` | `2 * precision * recall / (precision + recall)` | Return 0.0 if denominator is 0 |\r\n   134\t| `auc_roc` | Trapezoidal AUC over ROC curve (see below) | Requires at least one positive and one negative sample; return 0.0 otherwise |\r\n   135\t| `log_loss` | `-1/N * sum(y*ln(p) + (1-y)*ln(1-p))` | Clip probabilities to `[1e-15, 1-1e-15]` before log |\r\n   136\t\r\n   137\t**AUC-ROC computation:**\r\n   138\t\r\n   139\t1. Let `p_i` be the probability of the positive class for sample `i`.\r\n   140\t2. Sort all samples by `p_i` in **descending** order. Break ties by placing actual-positive sam"]
[47.200051, "o", "ples before actual-negative samples (this ensures a consistent curve).\r\n   141\t3. Walk through sorted samples, tracking cumulative TP and FP counts.\r\n   142\t4. At each sample, compute `TPR = cumulative_TP / total_positives` and `FPR = cumulative_FP / total_negatives`.\r\n   143\t5. Compute AUC using the trapezoidal rule: `AUC = sum over consecutive points: (FPR_{i+1} - FPR_i) * (TPR_{i+1} + TPR_i) / 2`.\r\n   144\t6. Include the origin point `(0, 0)` at the start.\r\n   145\t\r\n   146\t### 3.2 Multiclass Classification\r\n   147\t\r\n   148\t**Class list:** Collect all unique class labels from `actual` values, sorted in case-insensitive lexicographic order.\r\n   149\t\r\n   150\t**Per-class metrics (one-vs-rest):**\r\n   151\tFor each class `c`:\r\n   152\t- TP_c: `actual == c AND predicted == c`\r\n   153\t- FP_c: `actual != c AND predicted == c`\r\n   154\t- FN_c: `actual == c AND predicted != c`\r\n   155\t- TN_c: `actual != c AND predicted != c`\r\n   156\t- Precision_c, Recall_c, F1_c computed as in binary.\r\n   157\t\r\n   158\t**Aggregate metrics"]
[47.20017, "o", ":**\r\n   159\t\r\n   160\t| Aggregate | Definition |\r\n   161\t|-----------|------------|\r\n   162\t| `accuracy` | Total correct predictions / total predictions |\r\n   163\t| `precision_micro` | `sum(TP_c) / sum(TP_c + FP_c)` over all classes |\r\n   164\t| `recall_micro` | `sum(TP_c) / sum(TP_c + FN_c)` over all classes |\r\n   165\t| `f1_micro` | `2 * precision_micro * recall_micro / (precision_micro + recall_micro)` |\r\n   166\t| `precision_macro` | Arithmetic mean of per-class precisions |\r\n   167\t| `recall_macro` | Arithmetic mean of per-class recalls |\r\n   168\t| `f1_macro` | Arithmetic mean of per-class F1 scores |\r\n"]
[47.20028, "o", "   169\t| `precision_weighted` | Weighted mean of per-class precisions, weighted by class support |\r\n   170\t| `recall_weighted` | Weighted mean of per-class recalls, weighted by class support |\r\n   171\t| `f1_weighted` | Weighted mean of per-class F1 scores, weighted by class support |\r\n   172\t\r\n   173\tWhere **support** for class `c` is the count of samples where `actual == c`.\r\n   174\t\r\n   175\tDivision-by-zero: return 0.0 for any metric where the denominator is 0.\r\n   176\t\r\n   177\t### 3.3 Regression Metrics\r\n   178\t\r\n   179\tAll metrics computed from arrays of `actual` and `predicted` numeric values:\r\n   180\t\r\n   181\t| Metric | Formula | Notes |\r\n   182\t|--------|---------|-------|\r\n   183\t| `mse` | `mean((actual_i - predicted_i)^2)` | |\r\n   184\t| `rmse` | `sqrt(mse)` | |\r\n   185\t| `mae` | `mean(|actual_i - predicted_i|)` | |\r\n   186\t| `r_squared` | `1 - SS_res / SS_tot` | `SS_res = sum((actual - predicted)^2)`, `SS_tot = sum((actual - mean(actual))^2)`. Return 0.0 if `SS_tot == 0`. |\r\n   187\t| `mape` | `mean(|"]
[47.200388, "o", "actual_i - predicted_i| / |actual_i|) * 100` | Skip samples where `|actual_i| < 1e-10`. Return 0.0 if no valid samples. |\r\n   188\t\r\n   189\t---\r\n   190\t\r\n   191\t## 4. Report Format\r\n   192\t\r\n   193\t### 4.1 Evaluate Command Output\r\n   194\t\r\n   195\t**Markdown report** (`report.md`):\r\n   196\t\r\n   197\t```\r\n   198\t# Evaluation Report: <model_name> v<version>\r\n   199\t\r\n   200\tTask type: <task_type>\r\n   201\t\r\n   202\t## Summary\r\n   203\t\r\n   204\t| Metric | <split_1> | <split_2> | ... |\r\n   205\t|--------|-----------|-----------|-----|\r\n   206\t| <metric> | <value> | ... |\r\n   207\t\r\n   208\t(Metrics listed in the order defined for the task type. Values rounded to 6 decimal places.)\r\n   209\t\r\n   210\t## Split: <split_name>\r\n   211\t"]
[47.200501, "o", "\r\n   212\t### Dataset\r\n   213\t<dataset_name>\r\n   214\t\r\n   215\t### Metrics\r\n   216\t\r\n   217\t| Metric | Value |\r\n"]
[47.200616, "o", "   218\t|--------|-------|\r\n   219\t| <metric> | <value> |\r\n   220\t\r\n   221\t### Confusion Matrix\r\n   222\t\r\n   223\t(Classification only. Rows are actual, columns are predicted. Classes in lexicographic order.)\r\n   224\t\r\n   225\t|  | Predicted: <class_1> | Predicted: <class_2> | ... |\r\n   226\t|--|---------------------|---------------------|-----|\r\n   227\t| Actual: <class_1> | <count> | <count> | ... |\r\n   228\t| Actual: <class_2> | <count> | <count> | ... |\r\n   229\t\r\n   230\t### Per-Class Metrics\r\n   231\t\r\n   232\t(Multiclass only.)\r\n   233\t\r\n   234\t| Class | Precision | Recall | F1 | Support |\r\n   235\t|-------|-----------|--------|----|---------|\r\n   236\t| <class> | <value> | <value> | <value> | <count> |\r\n   237\t\r\n   238\t(Repeat \"## Split: ...\" section for each split, in the order they appear in the manifest.)\r\n   239\t```\r\n   240\t\r\n"]
