[3644.353658, "o", "# ML Evaluation Report Engine - Specification v3\r\n\r\n## Changes from v2\r\n\r\n"]
[3644.35375, "o", "1. **Conflict -- Metric Direction-Aware Regression Detection:** The `compare` command's regression detection logic has been fundamentally changed to account for **metric direction**. Metrics are now classified as either **higher-is-better** or **lower-is-better**. For higher-is-better metrics, regression means the metric *decreased* (as before). For lower-is-better metrics, regression means the metric *increased*. This replaces the v2 behavior where all metrics used the same comparison direction (`delta < threshold`). See updated Section 2.3 for full details including the new threshold interpretation.\r\n\r\n2. **Extension -- Split Filtering:** The `evaluate` command now accepts a `--split` flag to evaluate only specified splits. Multiple splits can be specified as comma-separated values.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Overview\r\n\r\n`mlreport` is a command-line tool that processes raw ML model evaluation data, computes\r\nclassification and regression metrics, generates structured reports, and detects\r\nperformance regressions betwe"]
[3644.353841, "o", "en model versions.\r\n\r\nAll computation rules, input/output schemas, and CLI semantics are defined in this\r\ndocument. The engine must support three task types: **binary classification**,\r\n**multiclass classification**, and **regression**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Data Model\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Evaluation Manifest\r\n\r\nAn evaluation manifest is a JSON file with the following structure:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"model\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"<string>\",\r\n    \"version\": \"<string>\",\r\n    \"task_type\": \"<binary_classification | multiclass_classification | regression>\"\r\n  },\r\n"]
[3644.353934, "o", "  \"evaluations\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"split\": \"<string>\",\r\n      \"dataset\": \"<string>\",\r\n      \"predictions\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"id\": \"<string>\",\r\n          \"actual\": \"<label_string_or_number>\",\r\n          \"predicted\": \"<label_string_or_number>\",\r\n          \"probabilities\": { \"<class_label>\": <float>, ... }\r\n        }\r\n      ]\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nField semantics:\r\n\r\n- `model.name`: Model identifier (alphanumeric + hyphens).\r\n- `model.version`: Semantic version string.\r\n- `model.task_type`: One of `binary_classification`, `multiclass_classification`, `regression`.\r\n- `evaluations`: Array of evaluation runs, one per dataset split.\r\n"]
[3644.354012, "o", "- `evaluations[].split`: Split name (e.g., `\"test\"`, `\"validation\"`).\r\n- `evaluations[].dataset`: Dataset identifier.\r\n- `evaluations[].predictions`: Array of individual predictions.\r\n- `predictions[].id`: Unique sample identifier within the split.\r\n- `predictions[].actual`: Ground truth label (string for classification, number for regression).\r\n- `predictions[].predicted`: Model prediction (string for classification, number for regression).\r\n- `predictions[].probabilities`: Class probability distribution (classification only). Keys are class labels, values are floats in [0,1] summing to ~1.0. Must be present for all classification predictions.\r\n\r\n**Validation rules:**\r\n- All required fields must be present.\r\n- `task_type` must be one of the three valid values.\r\n- For classification: `actual` and `predicted` must be strings; `probabilities` must be present.\r\n- For regression: `actual` and `predicted` must be numbers; `probabilities` must be absent or null.\r\n- Each split must have at least one prediction.\r\n- P"]
[3644.354084, "o", "rediction IDs must be unique within a split.\r\n\r\n### 2.2 Regression Configuration\r\n\r\nUsed with the `compare` command to define regression thresholds and rules:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"thresholds\": {\r\n    \"<metric_name>\": {\r\n      \"max_delta\": <float>,\r\n      \"max_relative_delta\": <float>,\r\n      \"severity\": \"<critical | warning | info>\"\r\n    }\r\n  },\r\n  \"rules\": {\r\n    \"max_critical_regressions\": <int>,\r\n    \"max_warning_regressions\": <int>\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n"]
[3644.354156, "o", "Field semantics:\r\n\r\n- `thresholds.<metric>`: Defines the acceptable change for a specific metric.\r\n- `max_delta`: Maximum acceptable absolute change in the regression direction. This is always a non-negative number representing how much worse the metric can get. For higher-is-better metrics: the metric regresses if `baseline - candidate > max_delta`. For lower-is-better metrics: the metric regresses if `candidate - baseline > max_delta`.\r\n- `max_relative_delta`: Maximum acceptable relative change in the regression direction as a non-negative fraction. For higher-is-better: `(baseline - candidate) / |baseline| > max_relative_delta`. For lower-is-better: `(candidate - baseline) / |baseline| > max_relative_delta`. When `|baseline| < 1e-10`, the relative check is skipped.\r\n- `severity`: How critical this metric is.\r\n- A metric is considered **regressed** if its actual change violates **either** the absolute **or** relative threshold (whichever is stricter).\r\n- `rules.max_critical_regressions`: Maximum number of c"]
[3644.354229, "o", "ritical-severity metrics that can regress. Exceeding this count -> overall FAIL.\r\n- `rules.max_warning_regressions`: Maximum number of warning-severity metrics that can regress. Exceeding this count -> overall FAIL.\r\n- Info-severity regressions are reported but never cause overall FAIL.\r\n- If a metric appears in evaluation results but not in `thresholds`, it is ignored for regression purposes.\r\n- If a metric appears in `thresholds` but not in evaluation results, it is ignored.\r\n\r\n**Backward compatibility for config files:** If a threshold entry uses the old field names `min_absolute_delta` and `min_relative_delta` (from v2), they should be treated as follows: `max_delta = -min_absolute_delta` and `max_relative_delta = -min_relative_delta`. The tool must accept both old and new field names. If both old and new field names are present for the same threshold, the new names (`max_delta`, `max_relative_delta`) take precedence.\r\n\r\n### 2.3 Metric Direction Classification\r\n\r\nMetrics are classified by direction:\r\n\r\n| "]
[3644.354306, "o", "Direction | Metrics |\r\n|-----------|---------|\r\n| Higher-is-better | `accuracy`, `precision`, `recall`, `f1`, `auc_roc`, `r_squared`, `precision_micro`, `recall_micro`, `f1_micro`, `precision_macro`, `recall_macro`, `f1_macro`, `precision_weighted`, `recall_weighted`, `f1_weighted` |\r\n| Lower-is-better | `mse`, `rmse`, `mae`, `log_loss`, `smape` |\r\n\r\n### 2.4 Per-Metric Regression Evaluation\r\n\r\nFor each split in both baseline and candidate manifests:\r\n\r\n1. Compute all metrics for both baseline and candidate on that split.\r\n2. For each metric defined in `thresholds`:\r\n   a. Determine the metric's direction (Section 2.3).\r\n   b. For **higher-is-better** metrics:\r\n      - `regression_delta = baseline_value - candidate_value` (positive means candidate is worse)\r\n      - The metric is regressed if `regression_delta > max_delta` OR `regression_delta / |baseline_value| > max_relative_delta` (when `|baseline_value| > 1e-10`).\r\n   c. For **lower-is-better** metrics:\r\n      - `regression_delta = candidate_value - baseli"]
[3644.354383, "o", "ne_value` (positive means candidate is worse)\r\n      - The metric is regressed if `regression_delta > max_delta` OR `regression_delta / |baseline_value| > max_relative_delta` (when `|baseline_value| > 1e-10`).\r\n3. A metric is counted toward the severity bucket if it regresses on **any** split.\r\n4. Each metric counts at most once toward its severity bucket regardless of how many splits it regresses on.\r\n\r\n**Comparison report deltas:** In the performance delta table and JSON output, the `absolute_delta` field is always `candidate - baseline` (unchanged from v2). The `relative_delta` is `(candidate - baseline) / |baseline|` (unchanged from v2). The direction awareness only affects the *regression detection logic*, not the delta display.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Metric Computation\r\n\r\n### 3.1 Binary Classification\r\n\r\n**Positive class convention:** The positive class is the class label that comes **last** in case-insensitive lexicographic order. For example, between `\"negative\"` and `\"positive\"`, `\"positive\"` is the positiv"]
[3644.354459, "o", "e class. Between `\"benign\"` and `\"malignant\"`, `\"malignant\"` is positive.\r\n\r\n**Confusion matrix** from raw predictions using the `predicted` field:\r\n- TP: `actual == positive_class AND predicted == positive_class`\r\n- FP: `actual == negative_class AND predicted == positive_class`\r\n- FN: `actual == positive_class AND predicted == negative_class`\r\n- TN: `actual == negative_class AND predicted == negative_class`\r\n\r\n**Metrics** (all rounded to 6 decimal places in output):\r\n\r\n| Metric | Formula | Division-by-zero handling |\r\n|--------|---------|--------------------------|\r\n| `accuracy` | `(TP + TN) / (TP + TN + FP + FN)` | Return 0.0 if denominator is 0 |\r\n| `precision` | `TP / (TP + FP)` | Return 0.0 if denominator is 0 |\r\n| `recall` | `TP / (TP + FN)` | Return 0.0 if denominator is 0 |\r\n| `f1` | `2 * precision * recall / (precision + recall)` | Return 0.0 if denominator is 0 |\r\n| `auc_roc` | Trapezoidal AUC over ROC curve (see below) | Requires at least one positive and one negative sample; return 0.0 otherwise |"]
[3644.354532, "o", "\r\n| `log_loss` | `-1/N * sum(y*ln(p) + (1-y)*ln(1-p))` | Clip probabilities to `[1e-15, 1-1e-15]` before log |\r\n\r\n**AUC-ROC computation:**\r\n\r\n1. Let `p_i` be the probability of the positive class for sample `i`.\r\n2. Sort all samples by `p_i` in **descending** order. Break ties by placing actual-positive samples before actual-negative samples (this ensures a consistent curve).\r\n3. Walk through sorted samples, tracking cumulative TP and FP counts.\r\n4. At each sample, compute `TPR = cumulative_TP / total_positives` and `FPR = cumulative_FP / total_negatives`.\r\n"]
[3644.354607, "o", "5. Compute AUC using the trapezoidal rule: `AUC = sum over consecutive points: (FPR_{i+1} - FPR_i) * (TPR_{i+1} + TPR_i) / 2`.\r\n6. Include the origin point `(0, 0)` at the start.\r\n\r\n### 3.2 Multiclass Classification\r\n\r\n**Class list:** Collect all unique class labels from `actual` values, sorted in case-insensitive lexicographic order.\r\n\r\n**Per-class metrics (one-vs-rest):**\r\nFor each class `c`:\r\n- TP_c: `actual == c AND predicted == c`\r\n- FP_c: `actual != c AND predicted == c`\r\n- FN_c: `actual == c AND predicted != c`\r\n- TN_c: `actual != c AND predicted != c`\r\n- Precision_c, Recall_c, F1_c computed as in binary.\r\n\r\n**Aggregate metrics:**\r\n\r\n| Aggregate | Definition |\r\n|-----------|------------|\r\n| `accuracy` | Total correct predictions / total predictions |\r\n| `precision_micro` | `sum(TP_c) / sum(TP_c + FP_c)` over all classes |\r\n| `recall_micro` | `sum(TP_c) / sum(TP_c + FN_c)` over all classes |\r\n"]
[3644.35469, "o", "| `f1_micro` | `2 * precision_micro * recall_micro / (precision_micro + recall_micro)` |\r\n| `precision_macro` | Arithmetic mean of per-class precisions |\r\n| `recall_macro` | Arithmetic mean of per-class recalls |\r\n| `f1_macro` | Arithmetic mean of per-class F1 scores |\r\n| `precision_weighted` | Weighted mean of per-class precisions, weighted by class support |\r\n| `recall_weighted` | Weighted mean of per-class recalls, weighted by class support |\r\n| `f1_weighted` | Weighted mean of per-class F1 scores, weighted by class support |\r\n\r\nWhere **support** for class `c` is the count of samples where `actual == c`.\r\n\r\nDivision-by-zero: return 0.0 for any metric where the denominator is 0.\r\n\r\n### 3.3 Regression Metrics\r\n\r\nAll metrics computed from arrays of `actual` and `predicted` numeric values:\r\n\r\n| Metric | Formula | Notes |\r\n"]
[3644.354807, "o", "|--------|---------|-------|\r\n| `mse` | `mean((actual_i - predicted_i)^2)` | |\r\n| `rmse` | `sqrt(mse)` | |\r\n| `mae` | `mean(\\|actual_i - predicted_i\\|)` | |\r\n| `r_squared` | `1 - SS_res / SS_tot` | `SS_res = sum((actual - predicted)^2)`, `SS_tot = sum((actual - mean(actual))^2)`. Return 0.0 if `SS_tot == 0`. |\r\n| `smape` | `mean(2 * \\|actual_i - predicted_i\\| / (\\|actual_i\\| + \\|predicted_i\\|)) * 100` | Skip samples where `\\|actual_i\\| + \\|predicted_i\\| < 1e-10`. Return 0.0 if no valid samples. |\r\n\r\n### 3.4 Bootstrap Confidence Intervals\r\n\r\nBootstrap confidence intervals provide uncertainty estimates for computed metrics. They are computed using the **percentile bootstrap** method.\r\n\r\n**Algorithm:**\r\n\r\n1. Let `N` be the number of predictions in a split and `B` be the number of bootstrap iterations (`--bootstrap` flag).\r\n2. Create a single PRNG instance seeded with `--seed` (default 42) using `math/rand`. This single PRNG is shared across all metrics and all splits -- it is **not** reseeded per metric or per s"]
[3644.354893, "o", "plit.\r\n3. For each bootstrap iteration `b` in `[0, B)`:\r\n   a. Resample `N` predictions **with replacement** from the split's prediction array using the shared PRNG.\r\n   b. Compute all applicable metrics on the resampled set.\r\n4. For each metric, collect the `B` bootstrap values and sort them in ascending order.\r\n5. Compute the lower and upper percentile indices:\r\n   - `lower_index = floor((1 - confidence) / 2 * B)`\r\n   - `upper_index = floor((1 + confidence) / 2 * B) - 1`\r\n   - Clamp both indices to `[0, B-1]`.\r\n6. The confidence interval is `[sorted_values[lower_index], sorted_values[upper_index]]`.\r\n\r\n**Applicable metrics for CI computation:**\r\n\r\n| Task Type | Metrics with CI |\r\n|-----------|----------------|\r\n| `binary_classification` | `accuracy`, `precision`, `recall`, `f1`, `auc_roc` |\r\n| `multiclass_classification` | `accuracy`, `f1_macro`, `f1_weighted` |\r\n| `regression` | `mse`, `rmse`, `mae`, `r_squared` |\r\n\r\nNote: `log_loss` and `smape` do **not** get confidence intervals. Micro and per-class metr"]
[3644.354972, "o", "ics do not get confidence intervals.\r\n\r\n**When `--bootstrap 0`:** Skip CI computation entirely. The `confidence_intervals` field must be omitted from the JSON output (not present as null or empty object).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Report Format\r\n\r\n### 4.1 Evaluate Command Output\r\n\r\n**Markdown report** (`report.md`):\r\n\r\n```\r\n# Evaluation Report: <model_name> v<version>\r\n\r\nTask type: <task_type>\r\n\r\n## Summary\r\n\r\n| Metric | <split_1> | <split_2> | ... |\r\n|--------|-----------|-----------|-----|\r\n"]
